[{:db/id "q-331463/field-1/5a8fc61ffc0ae000e86e9e1afba9edb424148024ababdf1b17a843073972ca08" :answer/id #uuid "d0175c04-e4da-5fc4-ba40-43c65c540cfd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{5}{s-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331463/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "0791a5d5-3615-5b0a-a2ec-01635434cc40" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-331463/field-1/5a8fc61ffc0ae000e86e9e1afba9edb424148024ababdf1b17a843073972ca08" :answer-field/choices ["q-331463/field-1/5a8fc61ffc0ae000e86e9e1afba9edb424148024ababdf1b17a843073972ca08"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-331463" :question/id #uuid "0460a9f5-0665-594f-936d-19b393b30df9" :question/math-academy-id "q-331463" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=3-5{e}^{2t}$ for $s>2.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{3-5{e}^{2t}} & =L{3}+L{-5{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =3L{1}-5L{{e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\n$$\nL{1}=\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-2},\\,s>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =3L{1}-5L{{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{s}-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{5}{s-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(s-2)-5s}{s(s-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(s+3)}{s(s-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{1}$ is defined for $s\\in (0,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (2,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(0,\\infty )\\cap (2,\\infty )=(2,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-331463/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad93e2e-20ab-5179-90b2-f96a357bd94c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331463"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-331464/field-1/573ee7bf3ffc48123e955b2a2edbc9be4ac7bca96543a87091c9c2d6c2eae09f" :answer/id #uuid "6bce9b3f-540e-5c17-978c-a8a464becb7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{3}{s-4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331464/field-1/817c879a7de8e9e73edf82244e219c7d4b4d4ecc9320a5a74e4d3a6ee68a26c9" :answer/id #uuid "d0d330e7-fdf3-56fa-b63a-d51e5d96ea23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{3}{s-4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331464/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "8d3a05bb-e570-55f2-b1bc-51b9a4ea3f4e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-331464/field-1/573ee7bf3ffc48123e955b2a2edbc9be4ac7bca96543a87091c9c2d6c2eae09f" :answer-field/choices ["q-331464/field-1/573ee7bf3ffc48123e955b2a2edbc9be4ac7bca96543a87091c9c2d6c2eae09f" "q-331464/field-1/817c879a7de8e9e73edf82244e219c7d4b4d4ecc9320a5a74e4d3a6ee68a26c9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-331464" :question/id #uuid "7560d20f-6a91-5fd6-b240-b4b3e7d11194" :question/math-academy-id "q-331464" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=1-3{e}^{4t}$ for $s>4.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1-3{e}^{4t}} & =L{1}+L{-3{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =L{1}-3L{{e}^{4t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\n$$\nL{1}=\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{e}^{4t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-4},\\,s>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{1}-3L{{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{s}-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{3}{s-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-4)-3s}{s(s-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(s+2)}{s(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{1}$ is defined for $s\\in (0,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{5t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (5,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(0,\\infty )\\cap (4,\\infty )=(4,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-331464/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad93e2e-20ab-5179-90b2-f96a357bd94c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331464"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-331458/field-1/d17292ea4c692607d9c0dada09979730ae33fd51aafff63dfbacb66dac406907" :answer/id #uuid "671ba222-548d-574c-8db1-826341a73a9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-5}-\\frac{1}{s-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331458/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2acd52f2-66ea-54c2-a7c0-7f7e5846291c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-331458/field-1/d17292ea4c692607d9c0dada09979730ae33fd51aafff63dfbacb66dac406907" :answer-field/choices ["q-331458/field-1/d17292ea4c692607d9c0dada09979730ae33fd51aafff63dfbacb66dac406907"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-331458" :question/id #uuid "c168ff21-8a4c-5e86-adf9-7cc64b86bf09" :question/math-academy-id "q-331458" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{5t}-{e}^{2t}$ for $s>5.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{5t}-{e}^{2t}} & =L{{e}^{5t}}+L{-{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{5t}}-L{{e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{5t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-5},\\,s>5 \\\\ L{{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-2},\\,s>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{5t}}-L{{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-5}-\\frac{1}{s-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-2)-(s-5)}{(s-5)(s-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s-2-s+5}{(s-5)(s-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(s-5)(s-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{5t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (5,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (2,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(5,\\infty )\\cap (2,\\infty )=(5,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-331458/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad93e2e-20ab-5179-90b2-f96a357bd94c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331458"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-89589"] :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-2t}-{e}^{4t}$ for $s>4.$" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-2t}-{e}^{4t}} & =L{{e}^{-2t}}+L{-{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-2t}}-L{{e}^{4t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+2},\\,s>-2 \\\\ L{{e}^{4t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-4},\\,s>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{-2t}}-L{{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}-\\frac{1}{s-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-4)-(s+2)}{(s+2)(s-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{(s+2)(s-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{(s+2)(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{-2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-2,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{4t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (4,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(-2,\\infty )\\cap (4,\\infty )=(4,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-331459/field-1/6e7d21fb219d056cb07ab386c7f722f7f2353ee8ad55e1a06e7fbc98c217fcca" :answer/id #uuid "e1067878-c74f-5fb2-8483-b56a92a0aba7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s+3}-\\frac{1}{s-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331459/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f1b607c5-ad2f-5abc-a187-f40febd6fc45" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-331459/field-1/6e7d21fb219d056cb07ab386c7f722f7f2353ee8ad55e1a06e7fbc98c217fcca" :answer-field/choices ["q-331459/field-1/6e7d21fb219d056cb07ab386c7f722f7f2353ee8ad55e1a06e7fbc98c217fcca"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-331459" :question/id #uuid "5b015829-58ae-5e70-bc83-8b1a18798403" :question/math-academy-id "q-331459" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-3t}-{e}^{t}$ for $s>1.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-3t}-{e}^{t}} & =L{{e}^{-3t}}+L{-{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-3t}}-L{{e}^{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+3},\\,s>-3 \\\\ L{{e}^{t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-1},\\,s>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{-3t}}-L{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3}-\\frac{1}{s-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-1)-(s+3)}{(s+3)(s-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{(s+3)(s-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{(s+3)(s-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{-3t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-3,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (1,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(-3,\\infty )\\cap (1,\\infty )=(1,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-331459/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad93e2e-20ab-5179-90b2-f96a357bd94c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331459"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-341109/field-1/11dd6481f0e40af4774ec8fd8834114c9131bc8902b1a3c28b6485cdd880840a" :answer/id #uuid "03a6d38d-800c-5224-a21c-b9070f907767" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341109/field-1/bf8e0cd7504194737d23ea4f81137d48336ff7aaca47ca8fec932b42fc66667b" :answer/id #uuid "8f890622-6fe9-51ca-bbb0-75f278dc4a8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341109/field-1/5a58a93bf13e30347bcef15ae2c41004b7c2c7969d1c25c2bf5d87550f223e27" :answer/id #uuid "92bcfa22-5788-585a-8ee7-e14f46a91c8f" :answer/type 